 ## Background
-The concept of empty space skipping is fundamental to efficient volume rendering, particularly for large-scale datasets where most of the volume is transparent or empty [G]. Early implementations relied on simple grid-based checks or octrees [13062-empty-space-skipping-via-acceleration-structure.md]. The use of hierarchical bounding box trees (BVH) for aerosol visualization was developed to handle large-scale smoke and fog [8182-hierarchical-bounding-box-tree-for-empty-space-skipping.md]. SparseLeap was introduced to address the fragmentation issues in standard octree-based skipping by decoupling the skipping data from the volume representation [5152-sparseleap.md]. The auxiliary terms for smoke mitigation were introduced in the context of view-dependent radiance fields to address shape-radiance ambiguity [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of empty space skipping is fundamental to efficient volume rendering, particularly for large-scale datasets where most of the volume is transparent or empty [G]. The auxiliary terms for smoke mitigation were introduced in the context of view-dependent radiance fields to address shape-radiance ambiguity [S1, unofficial].
 
